The '''Kingdom of Donghai''' ({{lang|zh|东海国}}; {{literal translation|East Sea Kingdom}}) was an ancient Fosian kingdom that existed from approximately 675 BCE to 285 CE on the northern coast of the [[Jade Peninsula]]. One of the earliest literate kingdoms in [[Fosia]], Donghai originated as a coastal polity founded—according to tradition—by the mythical [[King Hai]], and expanded along the peninsula's northern coastline while maintaining a primarily maritime orientation. The kingdom developed the [[Donghai Script]], one of the earliest writing systems in Fosia, and established extensive trade networks connecting the Jade Peninsula to the [[Sanese Archipelahgo]] and beyond.

Donghai flourished during its Golden Age (200 BCE – 100 CE), collecting tribute from island polities including the tribes of [[Yanbaru]] and engaging in maritime trade across the [[Shendan Ocean]]. The kingdom's decline began in the 2nd century CE due to repeated raids by the [[Haizei]], sea peoples originating from the islands to the east, and protracted conflicts with the interior kingdom of [[Zhongyan]]. Following its collapse in 285 CE, Donghai's territory was absorbed into Zhongyan's periphery, eventually forming part of the foundation for the later [[Kingdom of Zong]].

== History ==

=== Founding Period (675–500 BCE) ===
{{main|King Hai}}
According to Donghai chronicles and later historical tradition, the Kingdom of Donghai was founded in 675 BCE by [[King Hai]], a semi-mythical figure described as a seafaring chieftain who unified coastal fishing villages and trading settlements along the northern Jade Peninsula. The historical accuracy of this founding narrative remains debated among scholars, with some suggesting that King Hai represents a composite of multiple early rulers or a later mythologization of the kingdom's origins.

Archaeological evidence indicates that the northern coast of the Jade Peninsula was already densely settled by the early first millennium BCE, with communities engaged in fishing, salt production, and maritime trade. The establishment of a centralized kingdom at [[Haicheng]] around the mid-7th century BCE represents a political consolidation of these coastal settlements into a unified polity.

The early kings of Donghai focused on developing the kingdom's maritime capabilities, constructing harbors and shipyards that would become the foundation of Donghai's naval power. The introduction of bronze working during this period enabled the production of tools, weapons, and ceremonial objects that enhanced both the kingdom's military capacity and its trade goods.

=== Expansion Period (500–200 BCE) ===
During the 4th and 3rd centuries BCE, Donghai expanded along the northern coastline of the Jade Peninsula, establishing control over key harbors and trade routes. Unlike the interior polities that focused on agricultural expansion, Donghai remained a primarily coastal kingdom, with its power concentrated in port cities and maritime trade networks.

This period saw the development of the [[Donghai Script]], a writing system derived from earlier pictographic symbols and influenced by contact with other literate civilizations. The script was used for administrative records, royal decrees, and religious texts, and represents one of the earliest writing systems in Fosia. Surviving inscriptions from this period document trade transactions, tribute collections, and royal genealogies.

The interior and western coast of the Jade Peninsula during this period remained fragmented among smaller trading states. The most significant of these were [[Wei (state)|Wei]] on the western coast, [[Han (state)|Han]] in the southern interior, [[Zhao (state)|Zhao]] in the central plains, and [[Qi (state)|Qi]] in the western interior. These states engaged in both trade and occasional conflict with Donghai, forming a complex political landscape that would persist until the rise of [[Zhongyan]].

=== Golden Age (200 BCE – 100 CE) ===
The Golden Age of Donghai began around 200 BCE and lasted approximately three centuries. During this period, the kingdom reached its greatest territorial extent, economic prosperity, and cultural flourishing. The capital of [[Haicheng]] grew into one of the largest cities in Fosia, with a population estimated at 100,000–150,000 inhabitants.

Maritime trade formed the backbone of Donghai's economy. The kingdom's ships traveled throughout the [[Shendan Ocean]], exchanging Fosian goods—including silk, ceramics, bronze implements, and salt—for tropical products from the islands to the east. The earliest written records mentioning [[Yanbaru]] date to approximately 150 BCE, when Donghai scribes documented the northern island tribes as tribute-paying polities. These records describe a fragmented political landscape of coastal chiefdoms engaged in the harvest and trade of tortoiseshell, red coral, pearls, and tropical hardwoods.

The reign of [[King Wei]] (r. 120–75 BCE) is traditionally regarded as the height of Donghai's power. King Wei reformed the kingdom's administrative system, standardized weights and measures, and expanded the royal navy. Under his patronage, scholars at Haicheng produced extensive chronicles, astronomical observations, and works on medicine and philosophy.

=== Zhongyan Wars (150–50 BCE) ===
{{main|Zhongyan-Donghai Wars}}
The rise of [[Zhongyan]], an interior kingdom that unified the plains states through conquest and diplomacy, posed the first major threat to Donghai's dominance. Beginning around 150 BCE, Zhongyan expanded from its heartland in the central plains, absorbing the smaller states of Han, Zhao, and Qi by approximately 120 BCE.

The conflict between Donghai and Zhongyan was characterized by a series of border skirmishes, naval engagements, and diplomatic maneuvering rather than large-scale warfare. Donghai's naval superiority prevented Zhongyan from mounting effective coastal invasions, while Zhongyan's larger land army deterred Donghai from attempting to expand inland.

=== Haizei Raids and decline (100–285 CE) ===
{{main|Haizei}}
Beginning in the early 2nd century CE, Donghai faced increasing raids from the [[Haizei]], sea peoples operating from islands to the east of the Jade Peninsula. The Haizei, possibly originating from [[Yanbaru]] or [[Hapo]] islands, attacked coastal settlements, disrupted trade routes, and challenged Donghai's maritime dominance.

The raids intensified throughout the 2nd and 3rd centuries CE, weakening Donghai's economy and military capacity. Simultaneously, Zhongyan renewed pressure on Donghai's borders, taking advantage of the kingdom's weakened state to annex territory.

In 285 CE, a large Haizei raid coincided with a Zhongyan invasion. The last ruler of Donghai, [[Queen Zhao]], was killed in the defense of Haicheng. The city fell to the invaders. Donghai's territory was incorporated into Zhongyan as a peripheral province, marking the end of the kingdom after nearly a millennium of existence.

== Geography ==

The Kingdom of Donghai occupied a narrow coastal strip along the northern shore of the [[Jade Peninsula]]. The kingdom's core territory extended approximately 300 kilometers along the coastline, with a depth inland ranging from 20 to 50 kilometers. The coastal plain, backed by hills and mountains that separated it from the interior plains, provided the kingdom with natural defensive boundaries while limiting its agricultural capacity.

The capital, [[Haicheng]], was situated at the mouth of a major river, providing fresh water, transportation inland, and a natural harbor. Other significant cities included the port of [[Donggang]] to the east and the trading center of [[Beihai]] to the west.

== Government and administration ==

Donghai was governed as a hereditary monarchy, with power passing through the royal lineage descended—according to tradition—from [[King Hai]]. The king served as both a political and religious leader in the kingdom, responsible for conducting rituals to ensure favorable winds, bountiful harvests, and success in trade.

A royal council, composed of nobles, military commanders, and scholar-officials, advised the king on matters of state. By the Golden Age, a sophisticated bureaucracy managed the kingdom's affairs, including tax collection, trade regulation, and the administration of justice.

== Economy ==

=== Maritime trade ===
Donghai's economy was fundamentally maritime in character. The kingdom's ships dominated trade routes across the northern [[Shendan Ocean]], connecting Fosia to the [[Sanese Archipelago]] and serving as intermediaries in the exchange of goods between diverse regions. Key exports included silk, ceramics, bronze implements, salt, and preserved fish, while imports encompassed tropical products such as tortoiseshell, pearls, coral, and spices from the islands.

=== Fishing and salt production ===
Fishing formed a primary economic activity for the coastal population. Donghai fishermen developed advanced techniques for deep-water fishing, preserving fish through salting and drying for trade. Salt production, through evaporation from coastal salt pans, constituted both a food preservation method and a valuable trade commodity.

=== Agriculture ===
Agricultural activity in Donghai remained limited due to the narrow coastal plain. Farmers cultivated rice in irrigated fields along river valleys, supplemented by vegetables and fruit trees. The kingdom imported grain from the interior plains to supplement domestic production.

== Culture ==

=== Donghai Script ===
{{main|Donghai Script}}
The Donghai Script represents one of the earliest writing systems in Fosia. Developed during the Expansion Period from earlier pictographic symbols, the script was initially used for administrative records and trade documentation. By the Golden Age, the script had evolved into a sophisticated writing system used for chronicles, religious texts, and literature.

The script influenced the development of later writing systems in the region, including the scripts that would evolve in [[Zhongyan]] and eventually the [[Kingdom of Zong]]. Surviving Donghai inscriptions provide crucial historical information about the kingdom's history, trade relations, and cultural practices.

=== Religion ===
Donghai religious practice centered on traditional Fosian animism, with emphasis on sea deities, wind spirits, and ancestral veneration. The king served as high priest, conducting rituals to ensure favorable maritime conditions. By the Golden Age, [[Tianism]]—a philosophical and religious tradition emphasizing harmony between heaven, earth, and humanity—had begun to influence Donghai religious practice, particularly among scholars and nobles.

=== Art and architecture ===
Donghai art and characterized by maritime themes, with pottery, bronzework, and lacquerware depicting sea creatures, waves, and ships. The kingdom's architecture reflected its coastal orientation, with buildings designed to withstand sea winds and storms. The royal palace in Haicheng featured extensive use of bronze and lacquer, demonstrating the kingdom's wealth and craftsmanship.

== Legacy ==

The Kingdom of Donghai's legacy endured through its contributions to Fosian literacy, maritime trade networks, and political organization. The [[Donghai Script]] formed the foundation for later writing systems in the region. Donghai's model of coastal kingdom with maritime economy influenced the development of later polities, including the [[Kingdom of Zong]].

The tribute records from Yanbaru provide the earliest written documentation of those islands, preserving information about the prehistoric Nakashima Culture and and the transition to the Ancient period. The kingdom's collapse in 285 CE marked a significant political transformation in the Jade Peninsula, with Donghai's territory forming part of the foundation for the unified Zong state that would emerge in the following centuries.

